Wait: lastIndexOf(separator) finds separator ending at position... For multi-char separator, e.g. "alpha__beta", maxLength 7 gives cut "alpha__", lastIndexOf("__")=5>0 → slice(0,5)="alpha". Good.

Edge: cut index 0 means leading separator — can't happen since slug starts with letter/number. So `cut > 0` always effectively. Keep check per spec.

One subtlety: if limit falls at word boundary with separator starting at index maxLength (next char), cut has no separator → hard cut fine.

Another subtlety: "When the limit falls inside a word, cut at the last separator within the limit if one exists after position 0". If last separator inside the cut is at index > 0 but the cut isn't inside a word (i.e., cut ends exactly with separator, which we handle). Both produce same result anyway.

Test: "abcdefghij" max 5 → cut "abcde", lastIndexOf "-" = -1 → "abcde". Good.

Empty input: split gives [""] filtered → []. join → "". Good.

Also default options null? If options passed as null, `options = {}` default only for undefined; spec doesn't say; fine.

Let me write and run tests.

Also `String(input).normalize` — String handles null → "null" (has letters). Spec says convert input to string, so fine.

Combining marks: regex \p{M} with u flag. Good.

Also lowercase after removing marks. Turkish? NFKD then lowercase; fine.

Run test, plus quick ad-hoc checks for validation and examples.

