content.thinking:
Implement per spec. Careful details:

- separator validation: non-empty string else TypeError.
- maxLength: when provided must be positive integer; else TypeError. "When provided" — undefined? If options has maxLength: undefined, treat as not provided. Use `options.maxLength !== undefined`.
- NFKD, remove \p{M}, lowercase, keep \p{L}\p{N}, replace runs of others with separator. Note separator may contain regex special chars — avoid regex interpolation; use split approach.
- collapse repeated separators and trim.
- truncate: if slug.length > maxLength, cut to maxLength chars; if limit falls inside a word, cut at last separator within limit if one exists after position 0; otherwise hard-cut. Remove trailing separator left by truncation.

Implementation of truncation: `let cut = slug.slice(0, maxLength); if (slug.length > maxLength && !isSeparator boundary...)`. Rule: "When the limit falls inside a word"—that means char at index maxLength (next char) is not a separator and char at maxLength-1 is not a separator? Simply: find last separator index in cut; if exists and index > 0, cut at it (slice(0, idx)); else hard cut. But if limit falls at word boundary already (next char is separator), then last separator in cut likely at end? Actually cut at word boundary: e.g. "alpha beta", maxLength 5 → cut "alpha", next char is separator. Last separator in cut: none → hard cut, result "alpha". Correct without needing boundary check. If cut "alpha-" (maxLength 6), last separator index 5 > 0 → cut to "alpha". Then remove trailing separator. That's consistent.

But careful: rule says cut at last separator within the limit if one exists after position 0. So using lastIndexOf(separator, maxLength-1)... using cut string lastIndexOf works.

Separator may be multi-char, e.g. "__". slice-wise works with string lastIndexOf.

Then remove trailing separator: if result endsWith separator, slice off. But hard-cut trailing separator also removed. Also collapse? Result before truncation already collapsed.

Edge: maxLength smaller than... fine.

Non-ASCII separator regex concerns: use manual join.

Implementation:

```js
export function slugify(input, options = {}) {
  const separator = options.separator ?? "-";
  if (typeof separator !== "string" || separator.length === 0) {
    throw new TypeError("options.separator must be a non-empty string");
  }

  const maxLength = options.maxLength;
  let hasMaxLength = false;
  if (maxLength !== undefined) {
    if (!Number.isInteger(maxLength) || maxLength <= 0) {
      throw new TypeError("options.maxLength must be a positive integer");
    }
    hasMaxLength = true;
  }

  const parts = String(input)
    .normalize("NFKD")
    .replace(/\p{M}/gu, "")
    .toLowerCase()
    .split(/[^\p{L}\p{N}]+/u)
    .filter(Boolean);

  let slug = parts.join(separator);

  if (hasMaxLength && slug.length > maxLength) {
    slug = slug.slice(0, maxLength);
    const cut = slug.lastIndexOf(separator);
    if (cut > 0) slug = slug.slice(0, cut);
    if (slug.endsWith(separator)) slug = slug.slice(0, -separator.length);
